Subject: Cycle collection also runs at the reconcile safepoint; a reconcile no longer ratchets the threshold

What this does
  - quickjs.c js_drc_reconcile: after the parked values are drained and while the registers are pinned, if the heap is past malloc_gc_threshold (and the runtime is not in deferred mode) it runs the cycle collection (js_gc_auto_pinned: young or full as js_gc_auto decides) and rearms the pace. js_run_gc is split into the pin and drain part and js_gc_passes, which the safepoint path shares.
  - quickjs.c js_trigger_gc_slow: when a reconcile alone brought the heap under the threshold, the threshold is set to size + max(size / 2, 16 MB) directly; it no longer goes through js_gc_rearm_pace, which counted that reconcile as an unproductive collection and multiplied the step (up to 4x the heap) each time.
  - quickjs.c js_gc_rearm_pace: the growth step is at least 16 MB.
  - QJS_GC_STATS additionally prints the number of reconciles, the largest heap size seen at one, the last threshold, and how many collections each trigger path started.

Why we need it
  Compiled code that allocates from the nursery adds chunk bytes to malloc_size without going through the allocation threshold check, so the only collections were the teardown ones: deltablue piled up 1 GB of cyclic garbage. The reconcile safepoint runs often (the nursery trips it) and is a point where collection is as safe as in the allocation path. Separately, the reconcile-only branch fed the pace's unproductive streak, which raises the threshold by up to 5x per event.

Soundness
  The collection runs exactly as js_run_gc does after its own pin and drain (same state: registers pinned, parked lists drained, drc_in_reconcile cleared by the drain). Deferred mode keeps its rule (collect when the host asks).

Failure modes
  A collection at a safepoint in code that holds an object only in a native temporary that is neither pinned nor counted would free it; the allocation path had the same exposure. The 16 MB floor lets a small program hold up to 16 MB of cyclic garbage.

